## Problem

Fingerspelling practice tools rarely meet learners where they are—spoken language in, tactile ASL out. On the building side, many designers have not bridged the gap between digital and physical prototyping, so this project provides a case study for how to design and build a physical computing project with no prior mechanical engineering experience.

## Process

I integrated offline speech recognition with a servo array to spell letters in real time, iterating on hand ergonomics and latency. I had three weeks and no experience in mechanical engineering, so I relied on AI tools to teach myself about servo mechanics and hand ergonomics, and to generate CAD models for the hand. I also documented a LEGO-style pictorial build manual so non-engineers could reproduce the hardware without reading code.

## Outcome

A working prototype plus a pictorial manual for reproduction by non-specialists.

## What I Learned

Accessibility hardware needs documentation as a first-class deliverable—not an afterthought.
